    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given the temperature function \\( T(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50 \\), we need to find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees. This means solving the equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nTo simplify, we move 77 to the left side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies further to:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo make it easier to solve, we multiply every term by -1:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to factor this quadratic equation. We look for two numbers that multiply to give 27 and add up to -12. These numbers are -3 and -9. Therefore, we can factorize the quadratic as follows:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = (t - 3)(t - 9) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting the factors to zero gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt - 3 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t - 9 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving these, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad t = 9\n\\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value that satisfies the condition is 9. Therefore, the largest \\( t \\)-value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
