    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The temperature equation becomes $77 = -t^2 + 12t + 50.$ We would like to solve this quadratic for $t$ (by leading to the $\\text T$-value) which can be obtained by setting the equation equal to zero:\n\\[t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0.\\]\nThe quadratic formula states that given $a t^2 + b t + c = 0,$ the roots $t$ are given by\n\\[t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}.\\]\nFor our quadratic, we want $a = 1,$ $b = -12,$ and $c = 27.$ Plugging to the quadratic formula gives\n\\[t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144-108}}{2}.\\]\nSimplifying the radicand of the quadratic formula boils down to finding\n\\[t = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}.\\]\nPlugging back to the quadratic formula we find that the two roots are\n\\[t = \\frac{18}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = \\frac{6}{2}.\\]\nSimplifying the roots gives\n\\[t = 9 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = 3.\\]\nSince we want the largest time at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we can discard the smaller root and therefore obtain\n\\[t = \\boxed{9}.\\]\nNote: Another reasoning involves finding the critical points of the temperature function and determining which one is the highest, which will naturally correspond to the rate where the highest value occurs.",
